+- `searchFiles(root, query, { within } = {})` returns, sorted, the names of
+  the files directly under `root` (or under the subfolder `within`, relative
+  to `root`, when given) whose text contains `query`. The match is a
+  case-sensitive substring test; an empty query matches every file. `query`
+  and `within` must be strings. A `within` that leads outside `root`, whether
+  by `..`, an absolute path or a symbolic link, is refused with an error; one
+  that does not exist, or names a file, throws.
+
+Listing and searching look only at regular files directly under the folder:
+they do not descend into nested folders and they skip symbolic links, so a
+link planted under one user's root cannot reach another's. A search reads
+each file a chunk at a time and stops at the first match, so its memory use
+does not grow with the size of the documents; a miss still reads the whole
+folder.
 
